  {
    "date": "2023-10-01",
    "term": "母",
    "kana": "はは",
    "meaning": "(my) mother",
    "level": "N5",
    "pos": ["noun (common) (futsuumeishi)"],
    "priority": ["ichi1", "news1", "nf01"],
    "register": ["humble (kenjougo) language"],
    "pitch": [1],
    "stratum": "wago",
    "processes": ["derivation", "sound-change"],
    "headline": "Mother was papa, drifted to hawa, then a doubled sound pulled it back to haha in the late 16th century.",
    "morphemes": [],
    "sources": [
      {
        "quote": "From Old Japanese papa, in turn traced to Proto-Japonic *papa."
      },
      {
        "quote": "/haha/: */papa/ → /fafa/ → /fawa/ → /fafa/ → /haha/."
      },
      {
        "quote": "First cited to a portion of the Shoku Nihongi dated to 729, with the phonetic spelling 婆婆 (papa)."
      },
      {
        "quote": "Medial /f/ [ɸ] regularly changes to /w/ [ɰᵝ], resulting in /fawa/ → /hawa/, the expected final form; see hawa below. This form first appears in the Heian period. However, likely due to spelling influence or reduplication associations, the earlier /fafa/ resurfaced in the late 16th century towards the end of the Muromachi period, with both forms seen until recent times, when hawa falls into disuse. Initial /f-/ [ɸ] regularly becomes [h], resulting in modern [ha̠ha̠]."
      }
    ],
    "examples": [
