    "priority": ["ichi1", "news1", "nf06"],
    "pitch": [4],
    "stratum": "wago",
    "processes": ["compound", "sound-change"],
    "headline": "Imōto was imo + hito: a u-sound shift glued “sister” and “person” together.",
    "morphemes": [],
    "sources": [
      {
        "quote": "⟨imo₁⟩ → */imʷo/ → /imo/"
      },
      {
        "quote": "From Old Japanese 妹 (imo₁). First cited in the Kojiki of 712."
      },
      {
        "quote": "Speculatively, may be related to terms such as 女 (me₁, womi₁na, “woman”), 嫗 (omi₁na, “elderly woman”), and 母 (omo, “mother”), wherein the medial /-m-/ appears to signify woman, female. However, the difference in vowel values presents a difficulty."
      },
      {
        "quote": "/imo ɸito/ → /imowito/ → */imowuto/ → /imoːto/"
      },
      {
        "quote": "Originally a compound of 妹 (imo, “sister, close female companion”) + 人 (hito, “person”). First cited in The Tales of Ise of the early 900s."
      },
      {
        "quote": "This is an instance of ウ音便 (u onbin, “u sound shift”), found in terms with bilabial consonants (/m/, /b/, /w/) followed by i; commonly seen in terms such as 玄人 (kurōto, “professional”), 素人 (shirōto, “amateur”), or possibly, 相撲 (sumō, “sumo”)."
      }
    ],
    "examples": [
      {
