    "headline": "Mukō has two possible sources, both from a nominalization of the verb 向かう (mukau, “to face”).",
    "morphemes": [],
    "sources": [
      {
        "quote": "Two possible sources:"
      },
      {
        "quote": "* From a nominalization of the 終(しゅう)止(し)形(けい) (shūshikei, “terminal form”) or 連(れん)体(たい)形(けい) (rentaikei, “attributive form”) of the verb 向(む)かう (mukau, “to face”). The modern pronunciation would suggest that the morpheme boundary in the original verb muka.u has been lost."
      },
      {
        "quote": "* From a nominalization of the ウ音(おん)便(びん) (u-onbin, “u euphonic change”) form of 向(む)かい (mukai, the 連(れん)用(よう)形(けい) (ren'yōkei, “stem or continuative form”) of the verb 向(む)かう (mukau, “to face”)), compare dialectal forms like 向(む)こうて (mukōte)."
      },
      {
        "quote": "Accordingly, the historical kana spellings should be むかふ (mukafu) according to the former, and むかう (mukau) according to the latter. Compare 相撲(すもう) (sumō), of similar derivation."
      }
    ],
